WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.810
- A young adult presents to the office

00:00:01.810 --> 00:00:04.530
with body aches, fatigue, and joint pain.

00:00:04.530 --> 00:00:06.550
What happened next may surprise you.

00:00:06.550 --> 00:00:07.463
Let's get started.

00:00:08.330 --> 00:00:09.820
Before I walk in to see the patient,

00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:11.180
I actually do a chart review

00:00:11.180 --> 00:00:14.010
to see if the patient
has been seen before,

00:00:14.010 --> 00:00:15.180
what the vitals are,

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:18.130
what information has been shared
with my medical assistant.

00:00:18.130 --> 00:00:19.300
And I find out that the patient

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:21.940
has actually seen several other doctors

00:00:21.940 --> 00:00:24.410
over the last few weeks
for the same issue,

00:00:24.410 --> 00:00:26.830
and hasn't gotten a satisfactory answer,

00:00:26.830 --> 00:00:29.890
meaning a diagnosis or
treatment to solve the issue.

00:00:29.890 --> 00:00:30.970
In looking through the notes,

00:00:30.970 --> 00:00:33.260
I saw that the patient
was initially diagnosed

00:00:33.260 --> 00:00:34.780
with arthritis,

00:00:34.780 --> 00:00:38.230
that they attributed to the
patient being overweight.

00:00:38.230 --> 00:00:40.840
His BMI, I think, put
them into the category

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:42.120
of morbid obesity.

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:44.710
While that can cause osteoarthritis,

00:00:44.710 --> 00:00:46.360
he seemed to be a little bit too young

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:47.510
for that to start happening.

00:00:47.510 --> 00:00:48.640
I walk in to see the patient,

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:49.730
I introduced myself.

00:00:49.730 --> 00:00:51.370
One of the first questions is

00:00:51.370 --> 00:00:53.460
what has been the diagnosis thus far?

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:55.050
What have all the doctors told you?

00:00:55.050 --> 00:00:56.760
You went to see a doctor first

00:00:56.760 --> 00:00:59.090
who diagnosed a patient with arthritis

00:00:59.090 --> 00:01:00.470
related to his weight.

00:01:00.470 --> 00:01:02.200
The treatment for that was

00:01:02.200 --> 00:01:03.900
some over-the-counter medications,

00:01:03.900 --> 00:01:06.070
which I thought was a
rather incomplete treatment,

00:01:06.070 --> 00:01:07.870
even if that was the correct diagnosis.

00:01:07.870 --> 00:01:09.250
Then patient said when they went home

00:01:09.250 --> 00:01:10.760
and tried the over-the-counter options,

00:01:10.760 --> 00:01:12.400
the pain did not get better at all.

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:15.070
In fact, it got worse
and then a rash appeared.

00:01:15.070 --> 00:01:16.330
So the patient over the weekend

00:01:16.330 --> 00:01:18.270
went to an urgent care facility

00:01:18.270 --> 00:01:19.970
where they gave the patient a cream.

00:01:19.970 --> 00:01:22.100
When I investigated what the cream was,

00:01:22.100 --> 00:01:23.940
it happens to be a topical steroid

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:25.800
and the rashes did go away with the cream.

00:01:25.800 --> 00:01:29.730
However, over the period of about 10 days,

00:01:29.730 --> 00:01:32.610
the pain in the joints, in the muscles,

00:01:32.610 --> 00:01:34.450
specifically, the knees, the elbows

00:01:34.450 --> 00:01:36.070
started to return for the patient.

00:01:36.070 --> 00:01:38.807
So he came back to that
same doctor and said,

00:01:38.807 --> 00:01:40.800
"Hey, why am I not getting
better with the treatment,

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:41.650
what's going on?"

00:01:41.650 --> 00:01:44.510
And the doctor made some other evaluations

00:01:44.510 --> 00:01:46.930
and ended up sending
the patient for x-rays

00:01:46.930 --> 00:01:48.900
to see if there was anything going on

00:01:48.900 --> 00:01:51.340
from the imaging side of things.

00:01:51.340 --> 00:01:54.470
And again, reiterated to the
patient that the suspicion was,

00:01:54.470 --> 00:01:56.350
all the symptoms were coming from

00:01:56.350 --> 00:01:58.050
the fact that the patient was overweight.

00:01:58.050 --> 00:01:59.360
Clearly that wasn't the issue.

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:01.400
If you look at the course
of illness for this patient,

00:02:01.400 --> 00:02:02.760
the fact that it was getting worse,

00:02:02.760 --> 00:02:04.140
there's not only joint pain,

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:05.780
but there's also body aches.

00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:07.840
There's also fatigue setting in,

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:09.190
rashes starting to happen.

00:02:09.190 --> 00:02:11.230
These are multiple systems being involved

00:02:11.230 --> 00:02:13.320
and that tells me something
systemic is going on.

00:02:13.320 --> 00:02:15.060
I decided to ask the patient,

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:19.530
if he sent a message to any
of his friends of the rashes,

00:02:19.530 --> 00:02:20.560
typically we do that, right?

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:21.570
We're like, "Oh my God, what is this?"

00:02:21.570 --> 00:02:23.080
And we take a picture with our iPhones.

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:24.760
And to my luck, he did,

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:27.100
he took a picture of the rash
early on when it started.

00:02:27.100 --> 00:02:28.610
And it looked like an erythematous,

00:02:28.610 --> 00:02:30.720
meaning very red rash of the skin.

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:32.190
He only had a picture of one of them,

00:02:32.190 --> 00:02:34.340
but he said, all of them
looked quite similar.

00:02:34.340 --> 00:02:35.680
Now this is unique

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:37.960
because the fact that
it's not happening again

00:02:37.960 --> 00:02:38.890
in one area

00:02:38.890 --> 00:02:41.290
tells me that it's not an isolated

00:02:41.290 --> 00:02:43.120
or localized reaction to something.

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:45.360
If I have a reaction
right where my watch is

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:48.100
that tells me something
is going on in this area,

00:02:48.100 --> 00:02:49.870
either there's a contact dermatitis

00:02:49.870 --> 00:02:52.490
where I'm allergic to one of
the components of the watch,

00:02:52.490 --> 00:02:55.830
maybe a fungal infection from
trapping of the moisture.

00:02:55.830 --> 00:02:58.750
So the localized nature of the
rash would tell me something.

00:02:58.750 --> 00:02:59.820
But when we have a rash,

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:01.330
that's happening all across the body,

00:03:01.330 --> 00:03:03.220
that means something's
happening internally.

00:03:03.220 --> 00:03:05.930
Whether it's in the blood,
in the lymphatic system,

00:03:05.930 --> 00:03:06.950
I need to think further.

00:03:06.950 --> 00:03:08.867
So I started putting
pieces together and I said,

00:03:08.867 --> 00:03:13.867
"Wait, you have fatigue, joint
pain, body aches, a rash.

00:03:15.460 --> 00:03:17.310
Does this patient have Lyme disease?

00:03:17.310 --> 00:03:18.900
So I asked the patient,

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:22.350
does he spend a lot of times
outdoors, hiking, gardening?

00:03:22.350 --> 00:03:23.560
And he said, "No, absolutely not.

00:03:23.560 --> 00:03:24.530
I'm kind of a home body.

00:03:24.530 --> 00:03:25.390
I like to stay home.

00:03:25.390 --> 00:03:26.660
I don't really go outside much."

00:03:26.660 --> 00:03:27.493
And I'm like,

00:03:27.493 --> 00:03:28.326
"Do you have a dog?"

00:03:28.326 --> 00:03:29.580
And he's like, "Yeah, I love my dog.

00:03:29.580 --> 00:03:30.700
I sleep with my dog."

00:03:30.700 --> 00:03:32.220
I'm like, "Has your dog ever had a tick?"

00:03:32.220 --> 00:03:33.550
I see his eyes opened wide.

00:03:33.550 --> 00:03:35.470
And he says, "Yes, my dog,

00:03:35.470 --> 00:03:37.440
I actually pulled a tick off of him

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:38.550
not that long ago."

00:03:38.550 --> 00:03:40.050
The way that we get exposed to ticks

00:03:40.050 --> 00:03:43.120
is not only through being
outdoors and in tall grass,

00:03:43.120 --> 00:03:45.340
but through carriers or vectors.

00:03:45.340 --> 00:03:50.340
Like, Bear can literally run
into a big forest and area,

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:52.230
get a bunch of ticks on his fur.

00:03:52.230 --> 00:03:54.230
And him being brown, me not see it,

00:03:54.230 --> 00:03:55.500
climbs into my bed,

00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:57.210
leaves one of the ticks next to me.

00:03:57.210 --> 00:03:59.910
And they could easily start
feeding and cause an infection.

00:03:59.910 --> 00:04:02.220
Every other provider he seen thus far

00:04:02.220 --> 00:04:05.760
wrote them off as just an
allergy, needing some medications,

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:07.500
it's all due to his weight.

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:10.720
Weight is a problem for
a lot of my patients.

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:14.290
A lot of my patients do suffer
with their quality of life

00:04:14.290 --> 00:04:17.260
because they fall into the
category of morbid obesity.

00:04:17.260 --> 00:04:21.400
But it's also not right to
blame everything that's going on

00:04:21.400 --> 00:04:23.230
without doing a proper
history and physical.

00:04:23.230 --> 00:04:25.390
Like, that gets lost right then and there.

00:04:25.390 --> 00:04:27.490
So at this point we make a decision

00:04:27.490 --> 00:04:29.190
that I think what's going on

00:04:29.190 --> 00:04:32.380
is that the patient had
an erythema migrans rash.

00:04:32.380 --> 00:04:33.460
But what's unique is

00:04:33.460 --> 00:04:36.880
erythema migrans sometimes
appears with multiple lesions

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:37.850
throughout the body.

00:04:37.850 --> 00:04:39.800
And sometimes they're all completely red,

00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:42.360
and they don't all look like
the prototypical bullseye rash

00:04:42.360 --> 00:04:43.710
that we study in our textbooks.

00:04:43.710 --> 00:04:45.730
But then I started having to think

00:04:45.730 --> 00:04:49.870
the patient actually has early
disseminated Lyme disease.

00:04:49.870 --> 00:04:50.703
Why?

00:04:50.703 --> 00:04:52.730
Because he's starting to have joint pain,

00:04:52.730 --> 00:04:54.630
migratory arthritis,

00:04:54.630 --> 00:04:55.530
body aches,

00:04:55.530 --> 00:04:56.810
which is myalgias,

00:04:56.810 --> 00:04:58.530
basically pain in the muscles

00:04:58.530 --> 00:05:00.640
that means the inflammation is spreading.

00:05:00.640 --> 00:05:03.920
And unless we catch these
and act appropriately,

00:05:03.920 --> 00:05:07.060
it could lead to further Lyme, late Lyme,

00:05:07.060 --> 00:05:08.530
which is very problematic

00:05:08.530 --> 00:05:10.350
because then it could spread to your heart

00:05:10.350 --> 00:05:12.661
causing things like arrhythmias,

00:05:12.661 --> 00:05:14.210
AV blocks,

00:05:14.210 --> 00:05:16.250
neurologic complications.

00:05:16.250 --> 00:05:17.420
We had to act quickly.

00:05:17.420 --> 00:05:18.720
We drew blood then in there

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:20.280
to do two tests,

00:05:20.280 --> 00:05:22.170
one the ELISA test

00:05:22.170 --> 00:05:24.680
to make the initial
diagnosis of Lyme disease,

00:05:24.680 --> 00:05:25.980
and then a Western blot

00:05:25.980 --> 00:05:28.050
to do the confirmation test for it.

00:05:28.050 --> 00:05:31.000
And then right away prescribed
a course of Doxycycline,

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:33.160
which is an antibiotic that we like to use

00:05:33.160 --> 00:05:34.380
for the treatment of Lyme.

00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:36.230
Patient follows up two weeks later,

00:05:36.230 --> 00:05:37.450
what do we find out?

00:05:37.450 --> 00:05:40.400
A, his Lyme tests, both positive,

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:43.080
b, he feels a hundred times better.

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:44.210
Zero fatigue,

00:05:44.210 --> 00:05:47.040
zero joint pain, zero muscle aches.

00:05:47.040 --> 00:05:48.170
He's completely cured.

00:05:48.170 --> 00:05:50.520
Had he progress to late
stage Lyme disease,

00:05:50.520 --> 00:05:54.030
and had neurologic or
cardiac complications?

00:05:54.030 --> 00:05:57.000
He may have needed a hospital
stay for IV antibiotics.

00:05:57.000 --> 00:05:58.330
He could have lost his life

00:05:58.330 --> 00:06:00.360
as a result of a cardiac arrhythmia.

00:06:00.360 --> 00:06:02.020
If you happen to be a patient

00:06:02.020 --> 00:06:06.540
and you feel your provider is
overly focused on your weight,

00:06:06.540 --> 00:06:09.020
and you feel like you're
not getting good care

00:06:09.020 --> 00:06:10.180
because of that,

00:06:10.180 --> 00:06:11.460
you have two options:

00:06:11.460 --> 00:06:12.680
Option one,

00:06:12.680 --> 00:06:14.970
be direct point out to your provider

00:06:14.970 --> 00:06:17.750
that you feel like there's an
overemphasis on your weight.

00:06:17.750 --> 00:06:20.320
And while you know it is an
important factor in your health,

00:06:20.320 --> 00:06:22.400
it's not the sole
determinant of your health.

00:06:22.400 --> 00:06:24.450
And you'd like to have a
further discussion on that.

00:06:24.450 --> 00:06:25.760
If they're not receptive,

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:27.130
move to option two,

00:06:27.130 --> 00:06:29.420
or if you don't even wanna
have that conversation,

00:06:29.420 --> 00:06:31.340
that's your choice to move to option two,

00:06:31.340 --> 00:06:32.960
which is seek a second opinion.

00:06:32.960 --> 00:06:34.490
That is your right as a patient,

00:06:34.490 --> 00:06:38.320
and insurance cannot deny you
for seeking a second opinion

00:06:38.320 --> 00:06:41.080
because medicine is as much
art as it is a science.

00:06:41.080 --> 00:06:43.610
The relationship between
a provider and a patient

00:06:43.610 --> 00:06:44.890
has to be strong

00:06:44.890 --> 00:06:47.901
in order for a proper
health outcomes to occur.

00:06:47.901 --> 00:06:50.240
(ominous music)

00:06:50.240 --> 00:06:53.380
I wanna stress this super strongly.

00:06:53.380 --> 00:06:56.150
Be careful with certain health providers

00:06:56.150 --> 00:06:58.560
who make the diagnosis of a condition,

00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:00.660
which is not really a correct condition

00:07:00.660 --> 00:07:02.490
of chronic Lyme disease.

00:07:02.490 --> 00:07:05.640
They say that you need to
do all sorts of treatments

00:07:05.640 --> 00:07:07.360
like chelation therapies,

00:07:07.360 --> 00:07:10.440
extremely long courses of antibiotics

00:07:10.440 --> 00:07:12.400
that have no real evidence for them.

00:07:12.400 --> 00:07:15.840
And they blame a lot of
my patient's symptoms

00:07:15.840 --> 00:07:17.300
on this chronic Lyme disease,

00:07:17.300 --> 00:07:18.133
when in reality,

00:07:18.133 --> 00:07:19.230
that's not what's going on.

00:07:19.230 --> 00:07:20.620
If someone's pushing on you,

00:07:20.620 --> 00:07:23.450
expensive lengthy complex treatments,

00:07:23.450 --> 00:07:25.040
seek a second opinion

00:07:25.040 --> 00:07:26.880
either with another primary care doctor

00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:29.030
or even an infectious disease specialist,

00:07:29.030 --> 00:07:30.080
because there's a lot,

00:07:30.080 --> 00:07:32.850
a lot of fraud and malpractice
that happens in this area.

00:07:32.850 --> 00:07:35.440
And I've seen my patients truly suffer.

00:07:35.440 --> 00:07:36.990
That's just one of my patients' stories,

00:07:36.990 --> 00:07:39.020
but click here to check
out my bizarre life

00:07:39.020 --> 00:07:42.730
as an attending doctor as
always stay happy and healthy.

00:07:42.730 --> 00:07:44.162
Click here to check that out.

00:07:44.162 --> 00:07:46.745
(upbeat music)

